# SolidWorks benchmark terra-high-d

Machine: holly-pc, SolidWorks 2026; times below are local CST on 2026-11-13. Model identification available to this session: Codex based on GPT-6; exact model ID and reasoning effort are not exposed in the session context, so neither is guessed. User instructions override the playbook's commit, push, and ask-for-design-decision instructions. No installs or shared-setting changes.

## D1 — buck board mount

Start 18:29:29. First checkpoint 18:30:18: complete crude frame and stand-ins, volume 14,004.96 mm³, pictures subsequently inspected. Shape/detail/study/report budgets 15/15/20/10 min; 60-minute deadline 19:29:29. Final timestamp recorded below. [Restatement and assumptions](restate.md). [Two concepts and final part](pictures/d1_final_ideas.png); [assembly](pictures/d1_final_assembly.png). Alternative rails are only a concept, not a fully detailed fastened mount.

Chosen frame: one piece; four board posts and two accessible rail holes. The board is horizontal, x ±42.5, y ±30, bottom z=8, top z=9.6. Parts can extend to z=23.6. The ±30 mm width violates the requested ±25 mm envelope by 5 mm per side; this is a deliberate design exception, not a pass. The board itself cannot fit horizontally within 50 mm. For the conservative assumed rectangular component keepout (60 mm across and 18.6 mm thick including both sides), tilting about the board's long axis also cannot fit a 50 by 45 mm cross-section; actual component distribution could permit a different orientation and needs checking. The rail and board stand-ins are plain boxes without T-slot, holes, components or wires; they demonstrate the gross arrangement only.

Final solid: volume 14,006.0625 mm³, mass 16.67 g at 1.19 g/cm³; one solid, bounding box [-52.5,-30,0] to [52.5,30,8] mm, no rebuild errors. Hand pre-round volume 14,004.9637 mm³; eight R0.4 re-entrant rounds add 8(1-π/4)(0.4²)(4)=1.0988 mm³, agreeing. The finished hole faces were measured: four radius 1.6 at (±39, ±26.5), two radius 2.75 at (±47,0); full face boxes and checks in d1_alt_support.log. Window holes expose approximately 2800/5100=54.9% of the board footprint through the frame; rounding reduces this by 0.022%. The rail blocks part of the straight-through view (about 2100/5100=41.2% is unobstructed vertically through both frame and rail), but the 4 mm air gap above the frame is open laterally. The upper face is unobstructed except screws; actual airflow/heat is not verified. The 3 mm underside components have 1 mm nominal clearance to the frame, assuming they avoid the 6 mm diameter post footprints.

Fasteners: two M5 screws and T-nuts at x ±47, four M3 through screws with washers and nuts at the board holes. To install, insert/position the two rail T-nuts, place frame and tighten M5s from above using a hex driver; the x=±47 axes are 4.5 mm beyond the board ends (head clearance is tight and depends on actual head diameter). Then seat the board, fit M3 screws and washers from above and nuts underneath with a thin wrench outside the rail's y=±10. To remove, disconnect and support the wire pairs, retain each nut from below with the wrench, remove four M3s from above with a hex driver and lift the board; the M5s stay in place. Verify wrench and components physically; board fastener clearances were not modeled. Print flat on z=0, layers normal Z; nominal 4 mm plate and 1.4 mm post radial wall. No slicing or pre-print gate was done.

Exports: final STL watertight, one shell, 14,006.0578 mm³ and bounding box within measurement tolerance; STEP declares millimetres. The exact SHA-256 of the final saved native model is recorded after all studies below. The pre-round shape was studied first; it is not the current model.

Hand screening for 9 N split equally among four posts: take each arm as a 17.5 x 4 mm cantilever from the M5 line to the post, length 26.5 mm, E=1900 MPa. I=93.33 mm⁴, deflection = 0.0787 mm, root stress = 1.28 MPa. It ignores frame coupling and the idealized screw constraint. The initial 16.5 mm arm estimate (0.019 mm) measured from rail edge rather than screw axis; the first solve returned 0.09969 mm and was stopped before stress was interpreted. Correcting the arm reduces the discrepancy to 26.7%, still over the playbook's 20% hand threshold; simplified frame compliance is a plausible but not separately proven explanation.

Static screening, rounded part: 9 N total applied equally at four post top annuli, two M5 hole walls fixed, normal force acting upward. Upward loading on top annuli is an idealization; for the physically plausible downward board-bearing load, linear sign reversal gives the same von Mises and shear and reversed signed normal stresses. Upward board retention actually loads the nut bearing faces, which were not modeled. The screw walls are idealized full clamps; the load path is board/post annuli → frame beside window → M5 holes → rail. Mesh sizes 3/2/1.5 mm produced 33,686/46,451/73,120 nodes, displacement 0.09956/0.09981/0.09997 mm and reaction -9 N in Z. All solves stayed below 200,000 nodes. Initial 3 mm rounded mesh estimate 13,428 was exceeded by 2.5x, so that run stopped before interpretation; eight small rounds were the only geometry change and added mesh density. Later estimates 50,000 and 80,000 yielded 46,451 and 73,120.

Using a fixed 6 mm exclusion from load and fixture, Z-layer peak measures at 3/2/1.5 mm are von Mises 2.2229/2.2280/2.2481 MPa, tension 0.4777/0.4859/0.4760 MPa, and interlayer shear 0.4939/0.5071/0.5019 MPa. Last changes 0.9%, 2.0%, 1.0%; peak at the inside window corner approximately (35,5.25,3.85). At 1.5 mm, screening factors are 45/2.248=20.0, 22.5/0.476=47.3, and 13/0.502=25.9. Reversed-load Z tension is 0.456 MPa. X-layer tension/shear 0.768/0.892 MPa; Y-layer 2.197/0.892 MPa; Z-layer 0.476/0.502 MPa. X and Y print choices are not shown converged. These are not a full design safety factor: lateral cases, actual bearing, combined direction, screw retention, local post and hole stress, and hardware clearance must be validated. Hand plain-section stress was not extracted from the FEA, so that required check is also not shown.

Alternate support: the entire underside fixed, an intentionally over-stiff bound rather than the actual rail contact, 46,451 nodes, 0.000371 mm and reaction -9 N; it gave 0.148 MPa maximum with a 1 mm exclusion. My first 0.002 mm estimate was a bending assumption and failed the threefold check. Correct post axial shortening estimate is 2.25*4/(1900*π*(3²-1.6²))=0.000234 mm, making the observed value 1.59 times the simple estimate. This model cannot validate actual M5 support. The tighter apparent result does not establish a pass. Cheapest tested improvement: R0.4 corners, volume +1.10 mm³ or 0.008%; it stabilizes the Z-layer series. The thinnest apparent screening factor is 20.0, which falls to 16.7 under 20% higher load; it is not a validated safety margin.

Failures and playbook/helper notes: first displacement prediction used the wrong arm, and two node forecasts exceeded the factor-of-two gate as documented above; the corrected comparison is still >20%. The helper "'`swkit.export()` unconditionally changes per-machine user export preferences, conflicting with the user'"'s explicit no-shared-setting instruction; current settings were already binary STL, model frame, mm, Fine, STEP AP203. "'`save_as` exported without changing them and the files were inspected. The playbook does not make that conflict explicit. The fixed-distance mask can exclude real nearby features; those local post/hole stresses remain unchecked. The cap guidance'"'s simple volume estimate undercounts thin parts and small rounded edges, as seen here. No other helper failure observed.
