First full-shape checkpoint 18:40:15: [parts](pictures/d2_parts.png) and [pair](pictures/d2_pair.png) show the two halves, stepped seat and ring. Dimensions changed deliberately: shoulder Ø43.5 would encroach 0.25 mm radially on the Ø44 turning inner ring, so opening is Ø45 (0.5 mm radial clearance nominal, 0.425 mm if opening diameter is 0.15 mm small). The specified Ø6 counterbores on 5 mm centres overlap by 1 mm, and M3 screw heads of typical Ø5.5 would overlap 0.5 mm. Specified x=28.2 makes only 28.2−26−1.7=0.5 mm between clearance hole and seat (0.2 mm for the Ø4 insert), before tolerance. Moved M3 centres to x=±30.5, z=0 and 7: counterbores retain 1 mm between, and seat-to-insert radial wall is 30.5−2−26.075=2.425 mm at the split. Outside edge to counterbore is 2.5 mm. The existing ±24 mounting holes are retained. The M3 pattern is thus changed and mating hardware must use it.

Nominal seat Ø52.15, initial split faces y=±0.3 (0.6 mm gap), and seat z=−0.15 to 7.15 (0.3 mm axial float for the 7 mm ring). Free radial clearance is 0.075 mm; tightening can close the split through that clearance, retaining nominal 0.45 mm gap. With ±0.15 mm on printed seat diameter and exact Ø52 bearing, clearance ranges 0 to 0.15 mm radial; printed split faces independently ±0.15 mm give gap 0.3 to 0.9 mm. Worst-case 0.3 mm gap can just consume the 0.3 mm diameter clearance, leaving no guaranteed preload. Print and measure a coupon, size or shim the split and seat; actual clamp/preload is not established and x-side clearance may remain because the two arcs translate in Y. The 45 mm shoulder opening gives nominal radial clearance 0.5 mm to the inner ring and 1.5 mm radial land before the outer ring's Ø48 inside. Bearing steel ring seat axial clearance 0.15 mm each end; moving inner ring itself and seals were not modeled. Ring is Ø52/48 ×7 mm. Bolts need real M3 inserts selected and verified; no specified insert manufacturer's dimensions were available.

Upper volume 19,114.667 mm³, lower 19,838.967 mm³, ring 2,199.115 mm³, each one solid and feature-error free; bounds x ±36, z −4 to 11, upper y 0.3 to 32, lower −32 to −0.3. Hand volumes agree after correction. First script incorrectly halved the subtracted circular segment and logged 11,826.99 rather than 11,725.74 mm³ for the shoulder opening; the helper's 1% default tolerance let the 0.86% arithmetic error through. Corrected the script's analytical formula and independently recomputed both final volumes, which agree with the models exactly. This is a helper limitation: a generic tolerance can accept an incorrect hand calculation. Exports are watertight and one shell: STL upper 19,148.31 mm³, lower 19,868.94, ring 2,196.82; within 0.18% of native volumes and bounds within 0.034 mm.

Contact model uses one multi-body part: PETG halves; a Ø52/48 steel ring; four steel idealized screws, with Ø4 bonded plug in the insert hole, Ø3 free shank through Ø3.4, and Ø5.9 head bonded at the counterbore floor; and four very soft E=0.5 MPa pads bonded to the ring inner face and fixed at their inward ends. The lower y=−32 face is fixed. No screw preload, actual brass insert compliance or friction is included; the study represents initially clear, unpreloaded geometry, not a tightened joint. Both bearing-seat contact faces and both split-face pairs are no-penetration; loads are 300 N at the inner cylindrical ring face, rigidly distributed. Solver is Intel Direct Sparse. Study model and separate part artifacts are in the model folder; script and exact logs are here.

First +Y run mistakenly selected only the right split faces. It solved at 34,670 nodes and balanced the load but is INVALID for assessing both-sided split contact; it was rerun with two faces on each side. Corrected 4 mm mesh: +Y reaction (0,−300.002,0) N, displacement 0.15863 mm; +X reaction (−300,0,0) N, displacement 0.34205 mm; 34,670 nodes each. Fixed pad reaction is 0.784 N in Y and resultant 1.045 N in X (0.26% and 0.35% of 300 N respectively). A +Y bonded-seat twin moved 0.002224 mm, 71.3 times less than contact, with per-screw sampled tension about 2 N versus ~70–77 N for contact. The lower unloaded-side gap increases 0.1563 mm at the sampled ring/seat patch, supporting opening. Initial hand estimate +Y: 300/4 = 75 N per screw. Mean σy times Ø3 section area in a ±1.5 mm slice at y=10 gives 70.2, 71.3, 76.4, 77.5 N, sum 295.4 N; these are rough sampled nodal averages, not a validated triangulated section integration. Together with the 0.78 N pad reaction, this leaves about 3.8 N (1.3%) of 300 N unaccounted for. Thus the required 1% force-integration check and exact pad/seat force split are not yet demonstrated. The +X screw axial forces are about 14–17 N each; their difference from the naïve 75 N is because the circumferential contact/load path changes and must be checked by section forces, not guessed.
