Part one completed by 19:47:06, with [table written at 19:47:24](D3_part1_table.md) before editing. The [first-stage sheet](pictures/d3_part1.png) was opened: six discs, each with a bore and its A and counterbored B rings. Native volume agrees with the independent cylinder/annulus hand volume to the displayed precision for all six. Each is one solid with no rebuild errors, and each STL is watertight. The table lists mass, wall, and departures from the original. Cylindrical faces were individually matched by radius, centre, and lower/upper Z in "'`d3_build.log`; not just inferred from volume. The minimum-wall calculation compares all hole centres, their larger counterbore radii, the bore, outer rim and counterbore floor; it uses exact circles and is conservative at the chamfer.

The changed request was handled by opening each saved part and saving as `_b`, changing the existing `BASE` and `COUNTERBORE` driving dimensions, retaining the plane at the original bottom-measured floor, and adding `TOP_CHAMFER`. B through cuts were already through-all. Existing feature and sketch persistent references before and after were identical for all six; the full references are in `d3_edit.log` and `d3_part2.json`. P3'"'s A sketch was redrawn on PCD 70 at 22.5°: keeping the original 0° would leave only 0.3 mm between its A hole and the original B counterbore. A 1 mm chamfer would just meet the original P1 and P3 counterbores, leaving zero wall at the top. P1 B PCD was changed 60→58; P3 B PCD 84→82. The first P3 edit used PCD 80, then "'`d3_refine_p3.py` edited its B and counterbore sketches again to 82, a smaller departure that still leaves 1 mm to the chamfer. Its native feature references remained identical, and its volume and all hole faces were rechecked.

[Final table](D3_part2_table.md) gives CAD and hand volumes, masses, actual floor Z, minimum wall, and changes for all six. Thicknesses are 7.5/7.5/9.5/6.5/9.5/7.5 mm; floors are 3/3/4/1/3/3 mm from the bottom, unchanged from part one. Direct native-edge checks found radius R at z=top−1 and radius R−1 at z=top for every final chamfer (`d3_verify_chamfer_final.log`). All 12 STLs are watertight one-shell exports, their volume errors are below 0.11% and their bounds differ by at most 0.077 mm, under the playbook tolerances (`d3_audit.log`). Sampled points in each bore, A and B hole and immediately above/below every counterbore floor returned the expected solid/void result for all 12 STLs (`d3_inside.log`). The [final sheet](pictures/d3_final.png) was opened after the P3 correction; it shows six thicker chamfered discs and separate hole patterns.

The thinnest final wall is 1.0 mm (P1, P3, P4 and P6 at the chamfer/counterbore rim; P4 also has a 1.0 mm floor); 0.4 mm of material loss would reach the 0.6 mm minimum. Remaining design questions under the playbook'"'s one-diameter edge-distance guideline: first-stage B counterbore centre to outer edge is P1 5 vs Ø8, P3 6 vs Ø10, P4 6 vs Ø8, P5 8 vs Ø11, P6 6 vs Ø8 mm; after the chamfer it is P1 5 vs Ø8, P3 6 vs Ø10, P4 5 vs Ø8, P5 7 vs Ø11, P6 5 vs Ø8. P2 meets this particular check. The user-supplied outside diameters and interfaces were kept; loads, fastener head size, print tolerances and a physical fit are not specified or tested, so these distances need design review rather than a strength claim. The cheapest conservative improvement would increase outside radii to meet those edge-distance recommendations, at the cost of changing the specified envelope. No slicing or structural study was requested or run for these plates.

D3: no SolidWorks feature or edit failed. The first P3 B-circle location (80 mm) was a valid but unnecessarily large interface change, then improved to 82 mm. Model and export checks above are complete, with the stated design questions and no claim about a physical print.
